1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. Bxc6+ (5. d4 {Aitken-Saunders, Oxford Open 1971 (drawn in 24 moves)}) 5... bxc6 6. d4 exd4 {This line has been a favourite of mine for many years, though it is not mentioned in the latest MCO, and I suppose most players prefer 6...f6. The choice is between a free game at the cost of some temporary insecurity and a solid but cramped position, and is largely a matter of taste.} 7. Qxd4 ({Most of my opponents have played} 7. Nxd4 {but more recently the text move has been coming to the fore. The present game indicates it is more difficult for Black to meet.} c5 8. Nf3 Bb7 9. Nc3 Nf6 10. O-O Be7 11. Re1 O-O 12. Qd3 Nd7 {Broadbent-Aitken, British Championship 1948, but by capturing with the queen, White gains a tempo on this line.}) 7... c5 8. Qd3 Bb7 9. O-O Nf6 10. Re1 Be7 11. Nc3 O-O 12. Bf4 ({In three previous games I have played against the 7.Qxd4 line my opponents developed this bishop at d2 or b2. The text is much stronger as it commands the key squate e5 without loss of time.} 12. b3 {T.Beach-Aitken, British Championship 1954 (0-1)}) 12... Nd7 {The same position as at the end of the variation above except that White has got in Bf4. This position was also reached in Broadbent-Aitken (London 1948), but with Black to move. Jones, who is a keen openings student, has followed Broadbent's sound general plan but has gained a move by 7.Qxd4 instead of Nxd4.} 13. Rad1 Bf6 {I follow my usual procedure far too nonchalantly. 13...Qb8 is much more discreet and seems to me adequate.} 14. e5 Bxf3 $2 ({I had relied too much on this resource, but capturing the e-pawn would leave Black with a very inferior pawn formation.} 14... dxe5 15. Nxe5 Nxe5 16. Bxe5 Qxd3 17. Rxd3 Rfe8 18. Rde3 $14 {Kavalek-Teschner, Bundesliga 1981 (1-0)}) 15. Qxf3 dxe5 ({Only this way. If} 15... Bxe5 16. Bxe5 Nxe5 (16... dxe5 17. Qc6) 17. Rxe5) 16. Qc6 ({Too precipitate.} 16. Bg3 {, reserving all the threats, would probably have reaped the full reward of Jones' previous fine play. Now Black just escapes.}) 16... exf4 17. Rxd7 Qb8 18. Nd5 Qxb2 19. Nxf6+ ({The dashing} 19. Rxf7 Rxf7 ({not} 19... Kxf7 20. Qe6+ Kg6 21. Nxf4+ Kg5 22. Qd5+ {and White wins}) 20. Qxa8+ Rf8 {, etc.}) 19... Qxf6 20. Qxf6 gxf6 {My refusal of a draw at this point was not justified on the merits of the position and I was soon forced to recognise the illusory nature of my material advantage.} 21. Rxc7 Rac8 22. Ree7 Rb8 23. Kf1 Rb5 24. Ke2 Ra5 25. Kf3 {Sources: 1960 WECU Bulletin; BCM, May 1960, p135 (with identical annotations). "Aitken was lucky to scrape a draw against J. E. Jones, who produced a refutation of a variation favoured by the Scottish Champion for over twenty years." (BCM)} 1/2-1/2

